  "tour": {
    "title": "Romans 1: One Reading, Several Interpretive Lenses",
    "intro": "You’re asking whether Romans 1 is read pretty uniformly or whether real interpretive differences are at work. This tour starts with the chapter’s place in Paul’s argument, then lets you compare a conventional reading with a different account of God’s wrath, historical-context cautions, and a creation-order reading. It ends where Romans itself ends the setup: with Paul turning the accusation back on the person who is listening and judging.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:6bbfa166-com-20db375c-a035-35f2-8b38-516fd9b58eb7",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 180,
        "why": "Begin here for the map. The teacher calls Romans 1:16–17 “the thesis to the letter,” then explains that 1:18–32 presents the pagan world under condemnation before the argument widens. This gives you the broad shape that many readings share before the disputed details begin.",
        "episodeTitle": "An Overview of the Letter to the Romans",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/x82wrk/overview.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1242,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:5fe688cc-com-65c93edc-8ea4-3bbe-be46-a3936a1d3134",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 180,
        "why": "This is the clearest conventional exposition in the tour. The teacher says 1:18–3:20 is Paul’s “case against mankind,” and reads 1:18–32 specifically as the case against the “immoral pagan,” organized by the repeated refrain “God gave them over.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans 1:18-32",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/na8unf/romans_118-329cdz0.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1668,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:saved-from-gods-wrath",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 180,
        "why": "Now compare the meaning of wrath rather than simply assuming it means a standard penal picture. The speaker says God’s anger is “handing people over to a process that’s self-chosen,” and emphasizes that Romans presents rescue from that trajectory “right now,” with the cross foregrounding God’s love.",
        "episodeTitle": "Saved from God’s Wrath",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/2c920bb5-7256-4ada-b28c-d39f072152d5/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=2c920bb5-7256-4ada-b28c-d39f072152d5",
        "durationSec": 3397,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:34ccc8ab-com-40a66085-175b-3a5f-a31a-166e16baaf68",
        "startSec": 900,
        "endSec": 1200,
        "why": "This is the tour’s strongest historical-context counterpoint. Wright distinguishes the modern category “homosexuality,” calls it a nineteenth-century construct, and then argues from Juvenal, Plutarch, and Plato that the ancient world included “long-term partnerships” and “faithful, stable partnerships.” He does not simply say the passage is therefore irrelevant; he says that conclusion raises further questions about how to integrate Paul’s small passages with his wider theology.",
        "episodeTitle": "#162 Questions on sexuality and LGBT (Classic)",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR9904756994.mp3?updated=1749655920",
        "durationSec": 1756,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:619",
        "startSec": 3606,
        "endSec": 3720,
        "why": "Hear a different traditional reading of the sexual language, grounded less in idolatry-specific practice than in creation order. The teacher says Paul includes both male and female same-sex relations and that “at the heart” of the New Testament rationale is that this is “contrary to creation order,” while also distinguishing that from the older, procreation-based rationale he sees in Leviticus.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 79: Leviticus 17-18",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/12/NakedBible79_Leviticus17_18.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4631,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:585ee17e-com-d9a04cb3-54c1-335e-9d5a-2d2c6c6d5f9b",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 180,
        "why": "Finish with the safeguard built into Paul’s next move. After describing how chapter 1 “thrown the net incredibly wide,” the teacher says chapter 2 begins, “you are without excuse, every one of you who passes judgment.” That keeps Romans 1 from becoming a detached catalogue for condemning others: in this reading, the moralist who applauds chapter 1 is also being addressed.",
        "episodeTitle": "Romans 2:1-16",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/jkeic4/romans_21-16a7tgi.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1236,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "So the short answer is: the broad architecture is fairly standard—idolatry, God’s handing-over judgment, universal accountability, and the move toward the gospel—but the emphases are not identical. The clips disagree or differ over what “wrath” means, how ancient sexual practices relate to modern categories, and whether Romans 1’s sexual language is best explained through idolatry, creation order, or both. Romans 2 is the needed final check: whatever view you take, Paul does not leave the listener safely outside the indictment.",
    "totalSeconds": 1134
  },
